<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Igbo people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Igbo, Ibo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Igbo are one of the largest ethnic groups of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, living mainly in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and speaking the Igbo language. Historically most Igbo communities governed themselves through village assemblies, age grades and title societies rather than kingdoms, though Nri exercised wide ritual authority. Many Igbo were taken in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, and Igbo nationalism lay behind the secession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biafra">Biafra</a> in 1967.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/igbo-people.jpg" alt="Igbo people" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jerry Emeka Obi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Igbo_family_in_traditional_attire.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Igbo homeland straddles the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger River</a>, east and south of the Edoid and Idomoid groups and west of the Ibibioid cluster. The Igbo language belongs to the Niger-Congo family. Evidence of early iron smelting has been excavated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nsukka">Nsukka</a> region, dated to 750 BCE at Opi and 2,000 BCE at Lejja. Igbo-Ukwu, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anambra-state">Anambra State</a>, has yielded more than 600 prestige objects, including cast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-alloy sculptures and over 165,000 glass and carnelian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, dated to the 9th and 10th centuries. They may be associated with the Kingdom of Nri and its priest-king, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eze-nri">Eze Nri</a>, whose traditions trace the people back to a figure named Eri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Traditional Igbo government was in most places a consultative one. Communities were governed by councils of elders and assemblies of the common people, and many towns were also partly governed by the Ozo society, whose titles could be earned or inherited. A few places had kings, such as Onitsha with its Obi, or priest-kings, such as Nri and Arochukwu, and the spiritual though not political authority of the king of Nri was recognised across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igboland">Igboland</a>. Igbo unity as one identity is comparatively recent, and the novelist Chinua Achebe placed it somewhere between a tribe and a nation.</p><p>Many of the people taken across the Atlantic from the Bight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biafra">Biafra</a> were probably Igbo, usually sold to Europeans by the Aro Confederacy. Enslaved Igbo were known to planters as outspoken and rebellious, and were dispersed to colonies including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>. British colonial rule, which depended on appointed warrant chiefs, did not suit Igbo decentralised government, and the period was marked by conflicts. The Igbo embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and Western-style education.</p><p>After ethnic clashes in 1966 and 1967, General Emeka Odumegwu-Ojukwu proclaimed the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biafra">Biafra</a> on 30 May 1967, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigerian-civil-war">Nigerian Civil War</a> of 1967 to 1970. Millions of Biafran civilians died of starvation under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> by the Nigerian military, and Biafra was defeated and reintegrated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The traditional Igbo religion is Odinani, whose supreme deity is Chukwu, the &quot;great spirit&quot;, who created the world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and Odinani are now the major religions, with Islamic minorities. Yam is the staple crop, cultivated mostly by men. Traditional compounds group several houses around a central courtyard, built of mud, clay, wood and thatch. Instruments include the udu, a clay jug, the ekwe, a hollowed log, and the ogene, an iron bell.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Igbo people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nri lies in the northern <b>Igbo</b> heartland of south-eastern Nigeria, in what is now Anaocha local government area of Anambra State.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nri">Kingdom of Nri · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Igbo-Ukwu culture is an archaeologists’ name for the people who left three rich deposits in what is now the town of Igbo-Ukwu, “Great <b>Igbo</b>”, in Anambra State in south-eastern Nigeria.</blockquote><a href="/culture/igbo-ukwu-culture">Igbo-Ukwu culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Colonial governments ruled through chiefs, called the rulers they recognised paramount chiefs so that only their own monarch bore the title of king, as Britain did with the Swazi king from 1903, and appointed “warrant chiefs” where there had been none, as among the <b>Igbo</b> of south-eastern Nigeria.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aro emerged at Arochukwu in the late 17th century from wars and alliances among <b>Igbo</b>, Ibibio and Akpa groups, traditionally under leaders remembered as Nnachi and Akuma Nnubi.</blockquote><a href="/state/aro-confederacy">Aro Confederacy</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 17th century it was dealing with Portuguese, Dutch and English ships, buying captives from the <b>Igbo</b> hinterland through networks such as the Aro Confederacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/bonny-kingdom">Bonny Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kingship is believed to date from around the 15th century, when Benin influence spread east toward the river, and its traditions combine Edo and <b>Igbo</b> elements.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-agbor">Kingdom of Agbor</a></li></ul></section>
